---
import Chapter from '../Chapter.astro';

const domains = [
  { d: 'A', posture: 'Traditional', algs: 'RSA, ECDSA', tau: '(traditional, 0)', cls: 'classical', stage: 'traditional' },
  { d: 'B', posture: 'Hybrid', algs: 'Composite ECDSA + ML-DSA, RFC 10073', tau: '(hybrid, 0)', cls: 'hybrid', stage: 'hybrid' },
  { d: 'C', posture: 'Post-quantum', algs: 'ML-DSA-44, -65, -87', tau: '(post_quantum, 0)', cls: 'pq', stage: 'post_quantum' },
];

// Two checks run before the laboratory was built, to know what the standard
// validator of the platform can and cannot do.
const checks = [
  ['12 Nov 2026', 'OpenSSL 3.5 on Linux', 'Validates ML-DSA chains. Knows no composite signature algorithm. An intermediate as the only anchor needs the partial-chain option.'],
  ['13 Nov 2026', '.NET 11 X509Chain, Linux and Windows', 'Validates ECDSA P-384 and ML-DSA-65 chains and detects an altered signature in both. An intermediate as the only anchor gives PartialChain, which does not affect the use case: its anchor is the self-signed root of B.'],
  ['13 Nov 2026', '.NET 11 X509Chain, composite chain', 'On Linux it throws an OpenSSL exception when reading the composite public key and gives no verdict. On a preview build of Windows 11 the same program validates the chain.'],
];
---

<Chapter id="laboratory" num="12" kicker="Laboratory" title="Three real PKI domains, one reproducible laboratory">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Three EJBCA Community Edition instances on Proxmox, each a PKI domain with its own
      cryptographic profile, cross-certified with one another. <strong>Certificates are issued for
      real, and every constraint of <span class="m">Φ</span> is read from real extensions</strong>, never simulated.
      <span class="sn"
        ><b>How to read this section.</b> The rows that carry a date were run on that day, and everything else is described as planned work.</span
      >
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="tbl-wrap tbl-wrap--measure"><table class="tbl domains">
    <caption><b>Table 6.</b> Reference profile of each domain of the laboratory.</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Domain</th>
        <th scope="col">Posture</th>
        <th scope="col">Algorithms</th>
        <th scope="col">Reference floor <span class="m">τ<sub>d</sub></span></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        domains.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <span class={`dot dot--${r.cls}`} />
              {r.d}
            </td>
            <td data-label="Posture">{r.posture}</td>
            <td data-label="Algorithms">{r.algs}</td>
            <td data-label="Reference floor"><span class={`tag tag--${r.stage}`}>{r.tau}</span></td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The engine runs on .NET with Kestrel, over EJBCA for issuance and OpenSSL 3.5 underneath the
      platform validator, deployed with Ansible on Linux containers and with no proprietary
      licences. In the use case B starts with a traditional floor, so that it admits the clients of
      A, and raising it to its reference profile is the migration variant.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="tbl-wrap tbl-wrap--measure">
    <table class="tbl">
      <caption
        ><b>Table 7.</b> What the platform validator can do, checked before building the
        laboratory.</caption
      >
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Run</th><th scope="col">Subject</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          checks.map(([when, what, res]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nowrap" data-label="Run">{when}</td>
              <th scope="row">{what}</th>
              <td data-label="Result">{res}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The third row has a direct consequence, because every route of domain B starts at its root, which is
      composite, so on Linux the platform validator cannot give B its explicit validation. Domain B
      has to change format, validation library or system, and that decision is open (section 13).
    </p>
    <p>
      The evaluation measures and describes, and it claims no improvement in performance over existing validators. The selected
      path is checked against exhaustive enumeration on small topologies and against a reference
      validation library, and a negative result counts as a result.
    </p>
  </div>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .nowrap {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .dot {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 7px;
    height: 7px;
    margin-right: 0.6rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    vertical-align: 0.1em;
  }
  .dot--classical {
    background: var(--classical);
  }
  .dot--hybrid {
    background: var(--hybrid);
  }
  .dot--pq {
    background: var(--pq);
  }


  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .domains th:nth-child(3),
    .domains td:nth-child(3) {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
